    "FLAW_CLASS": 1,
    "ABSTRACTION_INDEX": 0.66,
    "DESCRIPTION": "In `bench/immune_agents.py`, `_verify_search_manifest()` does not actually verify that grep/glob operations were performed against allowed source files; it only checks regex syntax for grep and blindly accepts any glob. Evidence from code: `elif tool in (\"Grep\", \"grep\", \"rg\"): re.compile(pattern); verified += 1` and `elif tool in (\"Glob\", \"glob\"): verified += 1`. CT v2 confidence is then derived from `test_severity = man_verified`, so fabricated manifests with syntactically valid regex strings inflate confidence without any file grounding. This breaks the module's stated guarantee that CT v2 search manifests are 'mechanically re-verified'.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND: `bench/immune_agents.py`, function `_verify_search_manifest()`. Grep and Glob steps are counted as verified without tying them to actual source paths.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n        elif tool in (\"Grep\", \"grep\", \"rg\"):\n            # Check pattern is valid regex\n            pattern = args if isinstance(args, str) else str(args)\n            try:\n                re.compile(pattern)\n                verified += 1  # Pattern is at least syntactically valid\n            except re.error:\n                issues.append(f\"Invalid grep pattern: {pattern}\")\n\n        elif tool in (\"Glob\", \"glob\"):\n            verified += 1  # Glob patterns are hard to invalidate\n>>>>\n====\n        elif tool in (\"Grep\", \"grep\", \"rg\"):\n            if not isinstance(args, dict):\n                issues.append(f\"Malformed grep args: {args!r}\")\n                continue\n            pattern = str(args.get(\"pattern\", \"\"))\n            path_arg = str(args.get(\"path\", \"\"))\n            try:\n                re.compile(pattern)\n            except re.error:\n                issues.append(f\"Invalid grep pattern: {pattern}\")\n                continue\n            path_ok = (\n                path_arg in source_set\n                or os.path.basename(path_arg) in source_basenames\n                or any(str(p).endswith(path_arg) for p in source_paths)\n            )\n            if path_ok:\n                verified += 1\n            else:\n                issues.append(f\"Grep path not in allowed sources: {path_arg}\")\n\n        elif tool in (\"Glob\", \"glob\"):\n            pattern = args if isinstance(args, str) else str(args.get(\"pattern\", \"\"))\n            try:\n                matches = []\n                for spath in source_paths:\n                    if globmod.fnmatch.fnmatch(str(spath), pattern) or globmod.fnmatch.fnmatch(os.path.basename(str(spath)), pattern):\n                        matches.append(str(spath))\n                if matches:\n                    verified += 1\n                else:\n                    issues.append(f\"Glob matched no allowed sources: {pattern}\")\n            except Exception as e:\n                issues.append(f\"Invalid glob args {args!r}: {e}\")\n>>>>\n\nFOLLOW: This fix requires `_CT_V2_FALSIFIER_PROMPT` and `_build_ct_v2_prompt()` to specify structured `args` for grep operations, e.g. `{pattern, path}` rather than an opaque string; otherwise valid manifests will now be downgraded as malformed. Because `cytotoxic_t_cell_v2_shadow()` maps `man_verified` into `test_severity`, this change will reduce CT v2 confidence for underspecified manifests and may shift Helper-T synthesis outcomes. The logging in `_shadow_log.info()` remains correct but may show lower severity. VERIFIED: TRUE",
    "VERIFIED": true
  },
  {
    "FINDING_ID": "F003",
    "SEVERITY": 0.74,
    "FLAW_CLASS": 4,
    "ABSTRACTION_INDEX": 0.59,
    "DESCRIPTION": "In `bench/insect_brain.py`, persistence writes are not transactionally ordered and are non-atomic across related artifacts. Evidence: `persist()` first mutates in-memory state, then writes `round_XX.json` via `Path.write_text`, then writes `checkpoint.json` via `Path.write_text`. `signal_complete()` separately writes `completion_signal.json` via `write_text`. A crash between these calls can leave checkpoint state claiming a round exists while its per-round file is missing, or vice versa. This violates persistence-layer recovery consistency: after restart, `load_checkpoint()` trusts the checkpoint and reconstructs partial state without verifying corresponding round files.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND: `bench/insect_brain.py`, functions `_save_round_json()`, `_save_checkpoint()`, and `signal_complete()`. Writes use direct `Path.write_text()` and there is no transactional ordering across artifacts.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n        filepath.write_text(json.dumps(data, indent=2), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n>>>>\n====\n        self._atomic_write_json(filepath, data)\n>>>>\n\n<<<<\n        filepath.write_text(json.dumps(checkpoint, indent=2), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n>>>>\n====\n        self._atomic_write_json(filepath, checkpoint)\n>>>>\n\n<<<<\n        filepath.write_text(json.dumps(signal, indent=2), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n>>>>\n====\n        self._atomic_write_json(filepath, signal)\n>>>>\n\nAdd method to `InsectBrain`:\n<<<<\nclass InsectBrain:\n>>>>\n====\nclass InsectBrain:\n    @staticmethod\n    def _atomic_write_json(filepath: Path, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n        tmp = filepath.with_suffix(filepath.suffix + \".tmp\")\n        tmp.write_text(json.dumps(data, indent=2), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        tmp.replace(filepath)\n>>>>\n\nAnd in `persist()` reorder disk writes so checkpoint is written only after the round file succeeds:\n<<<<\n        self._save_round_json(record)\n        self._save_checkpoint()\n>>>>\n====\n        self._save_round_json(record)\n        self._save_checkpoint()\n>>>>\n(the call order stays the same; the substantive fix is atomic replacement for each file)\n\nFOLLOW: `load_checkpoint()` should be tightened to verify that every reconstructed `round_idx` has a corresponding `round_{idx:02d}.json` file before returning success; otherwise the system still trusts a possibly newer checkpoint than the round files. This also affects any external tooling tailing `completion_signal.json`: atomic replace avoids torn reads but consumers should handle file replacement semantics. VERIFIED: TRUE",
    "VERIFIED": true
  },
