    "DESCRIPTION": "In `bench/verification_chain.py`, `verify_inclusion_proof()` accepts any proof root supplied by the caller and only checks equality against an optional embedded root in the proof object; it does not bind the proof to a sealed epoch. Evidence: `build_inclusion_proof()` returns a Merkle root computed over all current records, but no epoch index or record_count is included. `verify_inclusion_proof()` simply verifies against the provided root string. If multiple epochs exist, a caller can validate a proof against a transient non-epoch root from an unsealed chain state. That is an interface correctness gap for a persistence layer that advertises epoch sealing.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND: `bench/verification_chain.py`, functions `build_inclusion_proof()` and `verify_inclusion_proof()`. Inclusion proofs are not bound to a sealed epoch artifact.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n    def build_inclusion_proof(self, index: int) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"Build an RFC 9162 inclusion proof for the record at `index`.\"\"\"\n        if not (0 <= index < len(self._records)):\n            raise IndexError(\n                f\"Index {index} out of range [0, {len(self._records)})\"\n            )\n        leaves = [_digest_bytes(r[\"chain_hash\"]) for r in self._records]\n        proof_steps = rfc9162_inclusion_proof(leaves, index)\n        root = rfc9162_merkle_root(leaves)\n        return {\n            \"index\": index,\n            \"chain_hash\": self._records[index][\"chain_hash\"],\n            \"proof\": proof_steps,\n            \"merkle_root\": \"sha256:\" + root.hex(),\n        }\n>>>>\n====\n    def build_inclusion_proof(self, index: int, epoch_index: Optional[int] = None) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"Build an RFC 9162 inclusion proof for the record at `index`.\n\n        If epoch_index is omitted, use the latest sealed epoch. Proofs are\n        always bound to a sealed epoch, not transient chain head state.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not self._epochs:\n            raise ValueError(\"Cannot build inclusion proof without a sealed epoch\")\n        if epoch_index is None:\n            epoch = self._epochs[-1]\n        else:\n            if not (0 <= epoch_index < len(self._epochs)):\n                raise IndexError(f\"Epoch index {epoch_index} out of range [0, {len(self._epochs)})\")\n            epoch = self._epochs[epoch_index]\n        record_count = epoch[\"record_count\"]\n        if not (0 <= index < record_count):\n            raise IndexError(f\"Index {index} out of range for epoch record_count {record_count}\")\n        leaves = [_digest_bytes(r[\"chain_hash\"]) for r in self._records[:record_count]]\n        proof_steps = rfc9162_inclusion_proof(leaves, index)\n        return {\n            \"epoch_index\": epoch[\"epoch_index\"],\n            \"record_count\": record_count,\n            \"index\": index,\n            \"chain_hash\": self._records[index][\"chain_hash\"],\n            \"proof\": proof_steps,\n            \"merkle_root\": epoch[\"merkle_root\"],\n        }\n>>>>\n\nFOLLOW: `verify_inclusion_proof()` should also validate `proof[\"merkle_root\"]`, `epoch_index`, and `record_count` against the supplied epoch or reject if absent; otherwise callers can still pass arbitrary roots. The CLI `prove-record` and `verify-proof` subcommands need argument/schema updates because proof JSON will now carry epoch metadata and default behavior changes from latest head to latest sealed epoch. VERIFIED: TRUE",
