    "DESCRIPTION": "In `bench/immune_agents.py`, `_verify_ct_claim()` and `skin_barrier_check()` both implement the same bounded file-read pattern by reading up to 50,000 lines into a list before checking one target line/window. Evidence: each function appends every line into `lines` until limit. This is not just duplication; it creates avoidable O(n) memory and time for a point lookup on every evidence item/finding. Since CT and skin barrier may run over many findings, the persistence-layer review target now incurs repeated full-file scans where line-window streaming would suffice.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND: `bench/immune_agents.py`, functions `_verify_ct_claim()` and `skin_barrier_check()`. Both read entire files up to 50k lines into memory just to inspect a cited line and nearby context.\n\nFIX:\nAdd shared helper:\n<<<<\ndef _verify_ct_claim(\n>>>>\n====\ndef _read_line_window(path: str, target_line: int, radius: int = 2, max_lines: int = 50000) -> Tuple[Optional[str], Optional[str], int]:\n    \"\"\"Return (actual_line, window_text, total_lines_scanned).\"\"\"\n    if target_line < 1:\n        return None, None, 0\n    start = max(1, target_line - radius)\n    end = target_line + radius\n    buf: List[str] = []\n    actual_line: Optional[str] = None\n    total = 0\n    with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for i, line in enumerate(f, start=1):\n            if i > max_lines:\n                break\n            total = i\n            if start <= i <= end:\n                buf.append(line)\n            if i == target_line:\n                actual_line = line.strip()\n            if i > end and actual_line is not None:\n                break\n    return actual_line, \"\".join(buf) if buf else None, total\n\n\ndef _verify_ct_claim(\n>>>>\n\nThen replace file-loading block in `_verify_ct_claim()`:\n<<<<\n    try:\n        lines: list = []\n        with open(real_path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            for i, line in enumerate(f):\n                if i >= 50000:\n                    break\n                lines.append(line)\n    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:\n        return False, 0.0, f\"Cannot read file: {e}\"\n\n    if line_num < 1 or line_num > len(lines):\n        return False, 0.0, f\"Line {line_num} out of range (file has {len(lines)} lines)\"\n\n    start = max(0, line_num - 3)\n    end = min(len(lines), line_num + 2)\n    window = \"\".join(lines[start:end])\n    actual_line = lines[line_num - 1].strip()\n>>>>\n====\n    try:\n        actual_line, window, total_lines = _read_line_window(real_path, int(line_num), radius=2, max_lines=50000)\n    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:\n        return False, 0.0, f\"Cannot read file: {e}\"\n\n    if actual_line is None or window is None:\n        return False, 0.0, f\"Line {line_num} out of range (file has {total_lines} lines)\"\n>>>>\n\nAnd replace analogous file-loading block in `skin_barrier_check()` with `_read_line_window()` to validate line existence without storing all lines.\n\nFOLLOW: This introduces a shared helper used by both skin barrier and CT verification, so any future line-limit changes must be made in one place. Because `skin_barrier_check()` only needs existence, it can ignore `window`; CT still uses both `actual_line` and `window`. Unit tests that asserted exact file-line counts from the old implementation may need updating because `total_lines` now reports scanned lines up to the configured cap rather than true file length beyond the cap. VERIFIED: TRUE",
