      "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND: `bench/insect_brain.py`, `read_context()`: docstring promises persistence-layer retrieval, implementation is memory-only.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n    def read_context(\n        self,\n        round_idx: int,\n        window: int = 3,\n    ) -> List[RoundRecord]:\n        \"\"\"Retrieve windowed context from persistence.\n\n        Returns the last `window` rounds of records. Does not return\n        full history — that lives in the persistence layer.\n\n        Args:\n            round_idx: Current round (for reference).\n            window: Number of prior rounds to include.\n\n        Returns:\n            List of RoundRecords within the window.\n        \"\"\"\n        start = max(0, len(self.state.round_records) - window)\n        return self.state.round_records[start:]\n====\n    def read_context(\n        self,\n        round_idx: int,\n        window: int = 3,\n    ) -> List[RoundRecord]:\n        \"\"\"Retrieve windowed context from in-memory state.\n\n        Returns the last `window` rounds currently loaded into memory.\n        This method does not read `round_XX.json` from disk; callers that\n        need persisted history must call `load_checkpoint()` first.\n\n        Args:\n            round_idx: Current round (for reference).\n            window: Number of prior rounds to include.\n\n        Returns:\n            List of RoundRecords within the loaded in-memory window.\n        \"\"\"\n        start = max(0, len(self.state.round_records) - window)\n        return self.state.round_records[start:]\n>>>>\n\nFOLLOW: This is a contract fix, not a behavioral change. It aligns documentation with current implementation and clarifies the required recovery sequence for callers. The usage example in the class docstring remains valid because it operates within one live run. No downstream code change is required, but any orchestrator assuming disk-backed lazy reads must be corrected externally.",
