      "DESCRIPTION": "Merkle inclusion proof verification accepts proofs against the current unsealed chain root rather than any sealed epoch root. In `bench/verification_chain.py`, `build_inclusion_proof()` always computes the root over all current records, and `verify_inclusion_proof()` accepts any supplied `merkle_root` string that matches the proof. Evidence: `build_inclusion_proof()` returns `merkle_root` from `rfc9162_merkle_root(leaves)` using all records; it does not reference `self._epochs`. This means a proof can verify against a transient in-memory root that was never epoch-sealed, despite module claims that epoch sealing provides compact inclusion proofs. The proof is cryptographically consistent, but not anchored to a sealed epoch, so the API overstates what it proves.",
      "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND: `bench/verification_chain.py`, `build_inclusion_proof()` ignores sealed epochs and emits proofs against the live chain tip.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n    def build_inclusion_proof(self, index: int) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"Build an RFC 9162 inclusion proof for the record at `index`.\"\"\"\n        if not (0 <= index < len(self._records)):\n            raise IndexError(\n                f\"Index {index} out of range [0, {len(self._records)})\"\n            )\n        leaves = [_digest_bytes(r[\"chain_hash\"]) for r in self._records]\n        proof_steps = rfc9162_inclusion_proof(leaves, index)\n        root = rfc9162_merkle_root(leaves)\n        return {\n            \"index\": index,\n            \"chain_hash\": self._records[index][\"chain_hash\"],\n            \"proof\": proof_steps,\n            \"merkle_root\": \"sha256:\" + root.hex(),\n        }\n====\n    def build_inclusion_proof(\n        self, index: int, epoch_index: Optional[int] = None\n    ) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"Build an RFC 9162 inclusion proof for the record at `index`.\n\n        Proofs are anchored to a sealed epoch. If `epoch_index` is omitted,\n        the latest sealed epoch containing the record is used.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not (0 <= index < len(self._records)):\n            raise IndexError(\n                f\"Index {index} out of range [0, {len(self._records)})\"\n            )\n        if not self._epochs:\n            raise ValueError(\"Cannot build inclusion proof without a sealed epoch\")\n\n        if epoch_index is None:\n            candidate_epochs = [e for e in self._epochs if index < e[\"record_count\"]]\n            if not candidate_epochs:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Record {index} is not covered by any sealed epoch\")\n            epoch = candidate_epochs[-1]\n        else:\n            if not (0 <= epoch_index < len(self._epochs)):\n                raise IndexError(\n                    f\"Epoch index {epoch_index} out of range [0, {len(self._epochs)})\"\n                )\n            epoch = self._epochs[epoch_index]\n            if index >= epoch[\"record_count\"]:\n                raise ValueError(\n                    f\"Record {index} is not included in epoch {epoch_index}\"\n                )\n\n        leaves = [\n            _digest_bytes(r[\"chain_hash\"])\n            for r in self._records[:epoch[\"record_count\"]]\n        ]\n        proof_steps = rfc9162_inclusion_proof(leaves, index)\n        root = rfc9162_merkle_root(leaves)\n        return {\n            \"index\": index,\n            \"epoch_index\": epoch[\"epoch_index\"],\n            \"record_count\": epoch[\"record_count\"],\n            \"chain_hash\": self._records[index][\"chain_hash\"],\n            \"proof\": proof_steps,\n            \"merkle_root\": \"sha256:\" + root.hex(),\n            \"sealed_at\": epoch[\"sealed_at\"],\n        }\n>>>>\n\nFOLLOW: `_cli_prove_record()` continues to work because the new parameter is optional, but CLI/help text should be updated if epoch selection is intended to be user-visible. Any existing consumers expecting proofs before `seal_epoch()` will now fail closed, which is consistent with the epoch-sealing contract. `verify_inclusion_proof()` remains API-compatible and can verify these richer proofs unchanged because it already checks the embedded `merkle_root` when present."
