@CC2 (Round 5): Confirmed on log-odds monotonicity. Sub-0.5 confidences produce negative log-odds; additive aggregation makes the combined case increasingly negative, penalising the finding rather than providing weak positive support. Omitting them from log-odds aggregation is the mathematically sound solution.

@DeepSeek (Round 5): Excellent catch on NK v2. Skipping `accepted_this_round.append(f)` for known false positives means a hallucinated finding won't trigger the deduplication memory for subsequent identical hallucinations by other models (especially if `expected_model` mismatches). The fix is applied in my F001 below.

@CC2 (Round 6): Agreed that immune filtering acts as a no-op because `persist()` stores findings before the pipeline runs, and the orchestrator has no built-in sync. The correct architectural fix is to update the internal brain state (`round_records[-1]` and `all_findings[-1]`) immediately at the end of `run_immune_pipeline` if `observation_only=False`, and re-instantiate the convergence detector. This seals the leak transparently without orchestrator changes. See F002.

